{"title":"Interval notation","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>interval notation</b> is another way to express an inequality. It use brackets [ and ] to represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/8/4d8f028cd4367a01aab496e90d43ab9e68bbbc53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\leqslant$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/c/a9cfd38d73296bbf6d99fa7892ecfef693b22939.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\geqslant$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" />, respectively. It uses parentheses ( and ) to represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/2/262a080d0d87e973b817945967a512346d8bfc49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&lt;$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/f/77f0cdbd0dbecd281387229d95a014017a0a395a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt;$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, respectively.\n</p><p>For example <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/4/16425fc741d5a70133e87dbc7717363ba9c9886a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\leqslant x&lt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" /> would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/8/ca8557a19c0cbe467743a9ef6b79e62a2a7fbf8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in[5,6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"20\" />. \nTo join multiple intervals, the <b>union symbol</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/c/a6cef8fd2bc2c7f9f1e27327b739f2cd7baa31ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cup$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />.\nAn interval with brackets on both ends is called a <b>closed interval</b> and an interval with parenthesis on both ends is called an <b>open interval</b>.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Infinities\">Infinities</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interval_notation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Infinities\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>Use infinities when you have an inequality like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820c190d962281aa2f9f8050cf0b21209c1a8653.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> where there is no other end. Infinities are <b>always in parenthesis</b>, not brackets. This is because infinity is not a finite, defined number that can be reached or closed off (as brackets indicate), so it cannot be \"included\".\n</p><p><b>Examples:</b>\n</p><p>The inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820c190d962281aa2f9f8050cf0b21209c1a8653.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/9/a7958efda0aa3fa6ded99b05b5a4ecd944bb321e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in(2,\\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"84\" height=\"20\" /> (it does not include <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />/starts at any number greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and goes to infinity).\n</p><p>The inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/0/6c0bbca8b172a3d1faf9fecdd6cf276f123f0236.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\ge 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d182bbd1ee0b2bb3560c16cfd03864f72b2b9e55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in[5,\\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> (it starts at/includes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and goes to infinity, which is closed off by a parenthesis).\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Brackets\">Brackets</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interval_notation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Brackets\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>Brackets mean that the interval includes the endpoint. It is used for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00c3863a5ca53a1be38db8921ae7b5bfbfbe1dc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ge$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/4/3b4cd01885cb569775f5d1143bc5139fff104bdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091701\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.012 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.025 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 87/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2998/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:35559:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018091701 and revision id 280245. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}